## Context

Author decision, 2026-11-09, on the weekly ticket audit's recommendation
("your recommendations as is"). Two worktree-cleanup guards cost more than
they protect and are retired together, because they share six files.

**1. "Ignored files count as residue" (ticket 1212).** The rule exists
because the harness's own `.gitignore` is `*` with an allowlist. Every other
project ignores build products, caches and environments, which is what git
means by ignored, and pays for the harness's special case:

| Ticket | What the rule blocked | Patch it needed |
|---|---|---|
| 1406, 1458 | JETP's seeded data directory | byte-compare against `.worktreeinclude` (`worktree_setup_copy.py`) |
| 1487 | ignored build output in review copies, three projects in one day | `--ignored-ok`, allowed only on `review-*`/`gate-*` names |
| 1495 | uv venv internals (`.venv/.lock`) | allowlist of venv entries; three gaze FINDINGS rounds |

**2. The `.tmp` citation gate (ticket 1119).** Before a worktree's `.tmp/`
is deleted, `worktree-tmp-guard.py` lists forge requests, verdict files and
branch commits for a citation of `.tmp`, and fails closed when it cannot
look. It held about 9.8 GB in arena over an id type check (1470). The
audit's search of journal and tickets found no block for a real citation
(weak evidence: blocks are not logged). The rule that evidence lives outside
`.tmp/` already covers the case, and `.tmp/` is git-ignored by registration.

## Decision

- A git-ignored file is disposable in every worktree removal path (gaze,
  verify-gate, roar, lair, molt, gc): `git status --porcelain` without
  `--ignored` is the residue rule. Tracked changes, staged changes and
  untracked files that are not ignored still block, as do the lock, liveness
  and unpushed-commit rails, which this ticket does not touch.
- `.tmp/` is ignored scratch like any other: no citation search, no forge
  call, before removal. `worktree-tmp-guard.py register` (the `/.tmp/` line in
  `info/exclude`) stays; a size line before removal may stay if it costs
  nothing.
- Risk accepted by the author: in the harness, a file outside the allowlist
  (the root, or a new top-level directory) is ignored, so such a draft is
  deleted with its worktree. The allowlist un-ignores whole working
  directories (`scripts/**`, `skills/**`, `tests/**`, `tickets/**`,
  `design/**`, `rules/**`…), so drafts there stay visible as untracked.
  No replacement guard.

## Sites (origin/main, 2026-11-09; grep again before starting)

Residue rule: `scripts/worktree-clean-lib.sh` (`worktree_residue`,
`worktree_scrub`, `_venv_disposable`, `_build_child_*`),
`scripts/worktree_setup_copy.py` (delete), `scripts/worktree-exit-preflight.sh`
(`--ignored-ok` and its name check), `scripts/worktree-gc.sh`
(`ownerless_tree_clean`), `scripts/gaze_close.py`, `scripts/gate_tree.py`,
`skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md`, `skills/gaze/references/cleanup.md`,
`tests/test_preflight_ignored_ok.py`, `tests/test_worktree_tools.py`,
`tests/test_gaze_close.py`, `tests/test_roar_close_and_clean.sh`.

Citation gate: `scripts/worktree-tmp-guard.py` (`preflight` and `removal`
modes), its callers `scripts/gaze_close.py:145`,
`scripts/worktree-exit-preflight.sh:62`, `scripts/worktree-gc.sh:259`,
`skills/gaze/references/cleanup.md`, `skills/molt/SKILL.md`,
`skills/roar/references/close-and-clean.md`, `skills/lair/SKILL.md`, and
`tests/test_worktree_tools.py`, `tests/test_forge_status_scripts.py`,
`tests/test_review_panel_cleanup_contract.py`,
`tests/test_roar_close_and_clean.sh`.

`.worktreeinclude` itself is the runtime's file and is not removed from any
project; the harness only stops reading it for cleanup.

## Coordination

Ticket 1496's request (another session's coder) edits
`scripts/worktree-tmp-guard.py` and `skills/molt/SKILL.md`. Branch this
ticket only after that request has landed.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] An ignored file never blocks a worktree removal, in any project; a test shows one ignored file passing and one untracked, unignored file blocking, through the preflight and through gc
- [ ] `worktree_setup_copy.py`, `--ignored-ok` with its name check, the venv and build-output allowlists and their tests are deleted; no caller is left
- [ ] No removal path calls the forge or searches for `.tmp` citations; `worktree-tmp-guard.py` keeps `register`; a test shows a worktree whose `.tmp/` holds files being removed with no forge available
- [ ] Skill and reference text that restates either rule is brought in line; the two retirements are separate commits, so either can be reverted alone
- [ ] The request body states the lines removed and added, by area
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Imagine (raid 91a35c)

Read on 998cd705, after PR 2367 (ticket 1496) landed: it added only an argparse
`usage=` string to `worktree-tmp-guard.py` and reworded `skills/molt/SKILL.md`.
No intent change; exit criteria untouched. Line numbers below are 998cd705's.

**Premise holds.** No hook, installer, Makefile, `bin/`, adapter or CI file
calls `worktree-tmp-guard`, `--ignored-ok`, `worktree_setup_copy`,
`worktree-clean-lib` or the preflight: every caller is in `scripts/` or
`skills/`. No rule file requires the old behaviour. gc's `[gone]` path
(`worktree-gc.sh:111-125`, `report_wip`) already uses plain status and has
never blocked on ignored files.

**Two costs the Decision implies and the body did not price** (raised to the
author, defaults applied meanwhile):

1. `docs/` is git-ignored staging by rule (`rules/zotero.md:23-26`) and holds
   research notes before they reach Zotero
   (`skills/reading-note/SKILL.md:78`). Where a project does ignore it, a note
   staged in a worktree's `docs/` and not yet stored goes with the tree; ticket
   1212's rule blocked that removal. (DOIfetch, zoteus, JETP-observer and
   search-works-for-zotero do not ignore `docs/`: there it still blocks as
   untracked.) In the harness, `docs/` is the only ignored path sessions routinely
   write deliverables to; the rest (`health/` but its journal, `projects/`,
   `build/`, `.panel/`, `.tmp/`, `.venv`, `.claude/`, settings files, caches)
   is scratch or machine state. Default: accept, and say at the rule's owner
   site (`skills/hunt/SKILL.md:125`) that evidence and deliverables never live
   in a git-ignored path. No guard.
2. An untracked, unignored `.venv` or `build/` (a venv without its own
   `.gitignore`, in a project that does not ignore it: ticket 1357) blocks
   again, as the Decision says untracked files do. uv, virtualenv and
   Python 3.13+ venvs ignore themselves and become disposable. Default:
   execute literally, state it in the request body, and let the affected
   project add the ignore line through its own tickets. The Blind-Spot seat
   found no affected project on padme (every venv found ignores itself or is
   ignored by its project). Ticket 1357 recorded seats answering this block
   with a forced removal: the rewrite of `skills/verify-gate/SKILL.md:133-139`
   keeps the no-force rule in words.

### Blind-Spot pass: 4 findings, both defaults above agreed as advisor

1. **The two retirements together give ticket 1495's dead-pid reclaim its full
   reach (verified).** `worktree-gc.sh:149-161` reclaims a tree on a branch
   that still exists upstream when its lock pid is dead, the tree is clean and
   HEAD equals upstream; commit 1242af86 counted on "the later rails (live
   cwd, .tmp guard, WIP)". The guard's removal mode refuses a branch not
   merged into the integration branch (`worktree-tmp-guard.py:325-330`), so
   today such a tree survives whenever it has a `.tmp/`. After this ticket it
   is removed. On padme, 2026-11-10, climate-finance-het holds three:
   `t2882-2750729-28445`, `t2880-2750729-32580` (`.tmp/` 72M) and
   `t0272-2750729-22569`, all on unmerged branches, clean, at upstream, owner
   pid dead. Committed work is safe on the branch; `.tmp/` and ignored state
   go. **Raised to the author before the merge**; default is the Decision as
   written ("worktrees are throwaway, branches hold durable state"). The
   request body lists, per project on this host, the trees the first gc would
   reclaim.
2. **"Every other project ignores build products, caches and environments"
   is narrower than what projects ignore.** climate-finance-het and
   JETP-observer ignore research data through DVC-written `.gitignore` files;
   each worktree's `.dvc/cache` is a symlink to the primary's, and a sample of
   40 pointer files and 4 directories found no only-copy data. chemin-de-voix
   ignores weights, checkpoints, runs and logs; AEDIST ignores experiment jobs
   and outputs: output produced in a worktree there would exist only there
   (latent: both have only a primary checkout today). Named in the request
   body; queued for the author with finding 1.
3. **No signal in the harm direction after merge.** A wrongful deletion
   leaves no trace, so "reopen on evidence" cannot fire. The seat's
   suggestion, a non-blocking line listing the tree's top-level ignored
   entries before removal, is more than the size line the Decision allows:
   queued for the author, not built.
4. **JETP-observer's `.worktreeinclude:1-3` restates the retired rule** ("a
   modified one is kept"). After the merge: a log line in that project's
   tickets, no edit from here.

Checked clean by the seat: adapters (Codex, Pi), hooks and the after-merge
runner hold no further caller; no served project has submodules or nested
worktrees; every removal site uses plain `git worktree remove`.

