### Simplest path

After the change the contract is `git status` plus `git worktree remove`
without `--force`, which refuses untracked and modified files and deletes
ignored ones. Callers of the guard's `preflight` and `removal` modes are
removed, not redirected; the size line is dropped. No new abstraction.

Rails a naive edit would weaken:
- keep `--untracked-files=all` and fail-closed `return 1` (a bare
  `status --porcelain` folds an untracked directory into one entry; an
  unreadable tree must not read as clean);
- keep `--no-optional-locks`, or the status call restarts the tree's idle clock;
- the guard's `removal` mode also checked HEAD against the branch and the
  integration branch, but only when `.tmp` existed: not a standing rail. gc's
  `[gone]` and upstream-equality checks, roar's `merge-base --is-ancestor` and
  `gate_tree`'s reachability check remain. Say so in the request body.

Commit split: retirement 2 first (pure deletion; `.tmp/*` is already tolerated
at lib:102), retirement 1 second. Three textually adjacent spots would make a
revert conflict (`worktree-exit-preflight.sh:104-111`, the `gaze_close.py`
docstring 12-13, `cleanup.md:21-23`): leave an unchanged line between the two
commits' edits and prove both `git revert --no-commit` runs on a throwaway
worktree. Keep the preflight's `--removal` and `--branch` flags accepted in
both commits (no-ops afterwards), or one commit's revert trips `set -u`; name
them as debt in the request body.

One request, two commits: mostly deletion, no symbol other tickets depend on.

### Tests

Each new test passes on the new code and fails on 998cd705. No test reads a
script's or skill's source to assert a string (author, 2026-11-10).

- Criterion 1, preflight: repo with `.gitignore` `*` plus an allowlist, an
  ignored `draft.md`: `--removal` exits 0 and `git worktree remove` succeeds;
  beside it an untracked, unignored file exits 1 and stays. Invert
  `test_worktree_tools.py:779` and `:1583[ignored-draft]`.
- Criterion 1, gc: through the owner-less or dead-pid lock path; the `[gone]`
  path already passes on old code and would prove nothing. Invert `:1770`,
  keep `:1736` as the negative case.
- Criterion 2: `--ignored-ok` exits 2 as an unknown option; "no caller left"
  is `make check` plus a grep quoted in the request body.
- Criterion 3: worktree with `/.tmp/` registered and a file in it, branch
  merged and gone, a forge-shaped `origin`, a `gh` stub on PATH that exits 1
  and logs its calls: gc removes the tree and the log is empty (old code
  retains it as "evidence unavailable"). Same case through close-and-clean
  after-exit and through `gaze_close`.
- `test_worktree_tools.py`: the citation-gate tests and the allowlist tests
  go; keep `:212`, `:1005`, `:1736` and one ignored-venv gc case; `:444` and
  `:1239` change (a missing exclude now blocks as untracked, exit 1).

### Later review

One week after merge, run gc and molt across projects and count trees still
skipped as not clean: a skip caused only by an untracked `.venv` or `build/`
names a project that needs an ignore line.

